#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wf-stamp — the producer for `contract-stamp`, which had six readers and none.

    wf-stamp --root <dir>              report every handbook's stamp state, write nothing
    wf-stamp --root <dir> --execute    write the stamp where there is not one yet
    wf-stamp --root <dir> --execute --restamp --authority <AMD record | audit run dir>
                                       ALSO overwrite a stamp that no longer matches —
                                       the authorized-amendment path, and only that
    ... --agent <name> [--agent <name>]
                                       only these handbooks. A name not yet registered is
                                       stamped in its staged draft, `staging/agents/<name>.md`
                                       (hire.md T1)

    wf-stamp --root <dir> --versions [--agent <name>] [--execute]
                                       set ORG-RECORD `workforce-version:` to the installed
                                       release and bump `handbook-version:` on every handbook
                                       stamped older (audit Step 5d item 3)
    wf-stamp --root <dir> --amended <AMD record> --agent <name> [--execute]
                                       record an amendment: bump `handbook-version:`, and the
                                       handbook reads UNRELEASED until --released
    wf-stamp --root <dir> --released <probe report> --agent <name> [--execute]
                                       record the re-probe PASS that releases it again

    wf-stamp --root <dir> --directives            report `.directives.sha` coverage, every
                                       extracted mirror against its live skill, and every
                                       handbook's `## Directives` sha pin against the file
    wf-stamp --root <dir> --directives --execute  write rows for blocks that have none,
                                       append a source block the mirror lacks, and re-pin
                                       the handbooks whose pin THIS run moved
    wf-stamp --root <dir> --directives --execute --restamp
                                       ALSO rewrite a row whose digest no longer matches,
                                       and re-pin a pin that was already stale

    wf-stamp --root <dir> --floor <handbook> --evidence <path> [--execute]
                                       record a handbook's measured length floor, which
                                       wf-conform reports instead of re-ordering the split

EVERY WRITE IS RECORDED in `.claude/workforce/stamp-ledger.jsonl`, one JSON object per line,
append-only: what was written, the value it replaced, the authority, and the argv. See
§ THE LEDGER below.

THE SECOND HALF, AND IT IS THE SAME DEFECT ONE FILE OVER. `.directives.sha` is the
sidecar `wf-protect-directives` compares every edit against and `wf-conform` re-hashes
at every `verify`. `checksums.md` § Where it runs names its path so that "a generator
with no named destination is not a generator" — and then no generator existed. This
file's own docstring below calls that out as the shape it was written to close for
`contract-stamp`, while `wf-protect-directives` line 24 claimed the gap was already
shut ("FALSE NOW ... The artifact exists"). The artifact existed because an audit run
composed it by hand, which is the hand-composed record `wf-apply --record-canary`
refuses on principle. Measured 2026-09-07 by `/workforce verify` on this repository:
two rows stale after the 1.24.0 install rewrote their files, three sacred blocks
carrying no row at all, and no shipped command able to write either.

SCOPE IS DECLARED, NEVER INFERRED, AND IT IS ONE FUNCTION. Blocks are collected over
`wf-census.sacred_scope`: every `.md` under `.claude/` except the run scratch
(`wf-census.SACRED_SCRATCH`: `workforce/work`, `workforce/staging`, `workforce/retired`), minus
anything `.censusignore` declares out. That is the exact set `wf-conform` demands a row
for, and it is a superset of the paths `wf-protect-directives` guards, so the hook can
never fire on a block the sidecar has no row for.

WHY THIS EXISTS. `contract-stamp` hashes a handbook's normalized `## Procedure` +
`## Verification` sections, and a recomputation that differs means the employee's
contract moved and its eval baseline is stale. Six shipped surfaces read it:

    org.md § Mode: `index` step 4     classifies CONTRACT-DRIFT from it
    verify.md § Org integrity         reports CONTRACT-DRIFT from it
    review.md step 1                  opens on recomputing it
    checksums.md § What is stamped    lists it as a stamped target
    amend.md, handbook.md             each say "recompute the contract-stamp"
    org-chart-format.md               documents the field and its drift meaning

NOTHING WROTE IT. `handbook-templates.md` ships the line as the literal placeholder
`<!-- contract-stamp: sha256:<of ## Procedure + ## Verification, normalized> -->`,
`checksums` is executor-followed and its directive-sidecar half is the half that ever
got performed, and no T-step in `hire.md` named a stamping action. So every reader
above compared a recomputation against a placeholder, and CONTRACT-DRIFT — the state
that says an employee's contract changed underneath its evals — was unreachable on
every org this product has ever built.

MEASURED 2026-09-05 by `/workforce verify` on playwright-mcp: six live employees,
five carrying the literal string `(set at registration)` and `engineering-lead.md`
carrying no stamp line at all. The sixth case matters on its own — the template line
is not even reliably emitted, so a producer that only replaced a placeholder would
have missed one handbook in six and reported a clean run.

This is the shape `audit.md` names for `.directives.sha` ("a generator with no named
destination is not a generator") and `wf-settings-apply` names for the settings write:
a consumer named and a producer assumed. It is the fourth recorded instance.

MISMATCH IS FLAGGED AND NEVER SILENTLY REPAIRED, per `checksums.md` § On mismatch: a
differing stamp is either a legitimate amendment that did not restamp or an edit
nobody authorized, and the hash cannot tell them apart. Re-stamping erases the only
evidence that something changed. So the default REFUSES to overwrite and exits 1, and
`--restamp` is the separate, explicit gesture `amend` makes when the amendment is the
authorization. Writing a FIRST stamp over a placeholder or an absent line is not a
repair — there is no prior claim to erase — so that happens under plain `--execute`.

GOVERNED ONLY, by the ORG-RECORD marker, the same discriminator `wf-conform` uses. An
adopted agent was censused into the chart with zero bytes changed
(`conversion-taxonomy.md` ADOPT); stamping one would write workforce's field into a
file workforce does not own.

THE STAGED COPY IS WRITTEN TOO, and this is not an extra. `wf-conform` asserts that
the staged draft still matches the registered bytes, and `hire.md` T6 verifies the
registered file's sha against the staged handbook's — so stamping one and not the
other manufactures a divergence in the very record that proves a registration was
verified. The stamp value depends only on `## Procedure` + `## Verification`, which
are identical in both copies, so both receive the same line and stay byte-equal.
The staged draft is the one `wf-conform` and `hire.md` T1 name, `staging/agents/<name>.md`;
the older `staging/<name>/HANDBOOK.md` is still read. A staged draft whose contract has
moved apart from the registered one (normal after any `amend`) is left alone, because the
registered handbook's digest would be a false claim about it.

THE LEDGER (odyssey-alive PL-U1, PL-U4; 2026-10-22). Until this file wrote one, nothing on
a host could say that a stamp had ever existed. `classify` gave the same UNSTAMPED to a
handbook that was never stamped and to one whose stamp line somebody deleted after editing
its `## Procedure`, and plain `--execute` then stamped the edited contract as if it were
new: the one piece of evidence that the contract moved, erased by the command that is
supposed to refuse that. A `--restamp` left no trace either, so a restamp run inside an
order was invisible afterwards. Now every write appends a line, an UNSTAMPED registered
handbook that the ledger or git HEAD shows was stamped before reads STAMP-REMOVED and is
refused exactly like a MISMATCH, and `--restamp` takes an `--authority` that must resolve
to an AMD record naming the handbook or an audit run directory, and is recorded with it.

UNRELEASED (odyssey-alive BB-U2). `amend.md` said an amended handbook is marked and not
dispatched to until it re-passes its probe, and nothing recorded that an amendment had
happened. `--amended` records it, `--released` records the PASS, and every report run
prints UNRELEASED for the gap between them and exits 1.

THE DIRECTIVE PINS AND MIRRORS (odyssey-alive 26, 27; nsayka-wawa DEF-Q-003). A handbook's
`## Directives` pins `.claude/workforce/directives/<skill>.md` by sha, and the employee's
first step STOPs on `DIRECTIVE-DRIFT` when the file no longer hashes to it. Nothing compared
the pin to the file between probes, and an extraction that appended to the file moved
every pin on it. The mirror itself was never compared to the live skill it was extracted
from, so a directive the user added afterwards never reached the employee, while
`INV-DIRECTIVES` reported it covered because the sidecar stamps the SOURCE. `--directives`
now measures both, appends what the mirror lacks, and re-pins what that append moved.
"""

import argparse
import errno
import glob
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import time

# CANONICAL GRAMMAR, COPIED VERBATIM FROM `wf-conform`, NOT IMPORTED. `checksums.md`
# rule 1 — "two readers of one artifact must share one grammar and one normalization,
# or they cannot both be right" — and this distribution expresses that by copying the
# reader with a comment naming its source, exactly as `wf-protect-directives` copies
# `BLOCK_RX`/`ROW_RX` from `wf-conform`. `bin/check` cross-tests the copies.
ORG_RECORD_RX = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*<!--\s*ORG-RECORD\s+START\b", re.I | re.M)
# THE SAME GRAMMAR `wf-conform` READS, deliberately. Two readers of one field must
# share one pattern, or a handbook can be tier 2 to one of them and tierless to the other.
TIER_RX = re.compile(r"tier:\s*(\d)")
ORG_RECORD_END_RX = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*<!--\s*ORG-RECORD\s+END\b.*$", re.I | re.M)

# The field, and every spelling of it that is on disk today: a real digest, the
# shipped template placeholder, and the `(set at registration)` form real orgs carry.
# Readers are liberal (`checksums.md` rule 1) — this matches the LINE, and the value
# is classified afterwards, so an unrecognised value is reported rather than skipped.
STAMP_LINE_RX = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*<!--\s*contract-stamp:\s*(.*?)\s*-->[ \t]*$", re.M)
DIGEST_RX = re.compile(r"^sha256:([0-9a-f]{64})$")

# Anchored on the line the stamp belongs after, so a first stamp lands in the same
# position the template puts it rather than at the top or bottom of the block.
OWNS_RECORDS_RX = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*<!--\s*owns-records:.*-->[ \t]*$", re.M)


def normalize(text):
    """Trailing whitespace and final newline. Same rule every reader here applies."""
    return "\n".join(line.rstrip() for line in text.strip().splitlines())


def section(body, heading):
    """Text under a HEADING — line-anchored, never a bare substring search.

    Copied from `wf-conform`, whose docstring records why: splitting on the first
    occurrence anywhere hands back the tail of the PREVIOUS section when the heading
    is also mentioned in prose, and a non-empty wrong segment passes every emptiness
    check. Returns None when the heading is absent — distinct from an empty section.
    """
    m = re.search(r"^" + re.escape(heading) + r"[ \t]*[^\n]*$(.*?)(?=^## |\Z)",
                  body, re.S | re.M)
    return m.group(1) if m else None


def declared_scripts(body, root):
    """[(project-relative path, sha256 of its bytes or None if absent)] for every script a
    declared `Check:` or `Negative:` in `## Verification` runs, sorted and deduplicated.

    THE CHECK IS WHAT THE SCRIPT DOES, NOT WHAT THE BULLET SAYS (apps-odyssey-alive DEF-Q-22,
    2026-09-16). A byte-density split moved 26 long check commands into
    `.claude/workforce/checks/<employee>/*.sh` and left each bullet reading `bash <script>`,
    so an edit inside a script changed what the check does and the stamp, which hashed only
    the bullet text, could not see it.

    WHICH BULLETS AND WHICH SCRIPT ARE `wf-checkrun`'S ANSWER, imported, never restated: the
    same masking (a `Check:` shown inside a fence or comment is not declared), the same
    section locator, and `wf-census.Census.resolve_hook_command`, the one resolver that
    unwraps `bash <path>` and its relatives and calls an inline `bash -c` or a bare PATH
    name undecidable. So the scripts folded here are exactly the ones `wf-checkrun` reports
    as `resolved` or `dead`. Only a script INSIDE the project is folded: a path under an
    install or `$HOME` differs per machine and is not this handbook's contract. A declared
    script that is not on disk is folded as absent, so deleting it is drift too. Only the
    script a bullet names is read, never what that script sources in turn.
    """
    if root is None:
        return []
    checkrun = _load_sibling("wf-checkrun")
    masked, _ambiguous = checkrun.mask_regions(body)
    span = checkrun.section_span(masked)
    if not isinstance(span, tuple):
        return []
    decl = masked[span[0]:span[1]]
    cmds = checkrun.CHECK_RX.findall(decl) + checkrun.NEGATIVE_RX.findall(decl)
    if not cmds:
        return []
    census = _load_sibling("wf-census").Census(root)
    real_root = os.path.realpath(root)
    out = {}
    for cmd in cmds:
        script, exists = census.resolve_hook_command(cmd)
        if script is None:
            continue
        real = os.path.realpath(script)
        if not real.startswith(real_root + os.sep):
            continue
        rel = os.path.relpath(real, real_root).replace(os.sep, "/")
        sha = None
        if exists and os.path.isfile(real):
            try:
                sha = hashlib.sha256(open(real, "rb").read()).hexdigest()
            except OSError:
                sha = None
        out[rel] = sha
    return sorted(out.items())


def contract_digest(body, root=None, scripts=None):
    """sha256 over the normalized `## Procedure` + `## Verification` sections, plus the
    bytes of every script a declared check in `## Verification` runs (`declared_scripts`).

    Returns None when either section is absent: a handbook missing one of them has no
    contract to stamp, and `wf-conform` already owns that verdict. The two normalized
    sections are joined by a single newline so that moving a line from the end of one
    to the start of the other CHANGES the digest — concatenating them bare would make
    that edit invisible, and it is exactly the kind of edit a drift check exists for.

    A HANDBOOK THAT CALLS NO SCRIPT HASHES EXACTLY AS IT DID BEFORE SCRIPTS WERE FOLDED, so
    the change moves no stamp that has nothing new to cover. Each script adds one line,
    `script:<path>:<sha256 or ABSENT>`. `root=None` gives the pre-fold text-only digest,
    which `classify` uses to recognise a stamp an older release wrote.
    """
    proc, ver = section(body, "## Procedure"), section(body, "## Verification")
    if proc is None or ver is None:
        return None
    payload = normalize(proc) + "\n" + normalize(ver)
    if scripts is None:
        scripts = declared_scripts(body, root)
    for rel, sha in scripts:
        payload += f"\nscript:{rel}:{sha or 'ABSENT'}"
    return hashlib.sha256(payload.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


def classify(body, root=None):
    """(state, recomputed, recorded) for one handbook body.

    States: NO-RECORD (not governed), N/A (a delegating tier, which correctly has no
    procedure contract), NO-SECTIONS, UNSTAMPED (placeholder or no line at all), OK,
    PRE-FOLD, MISMATCH.

    PRE-FOLD is a stamp an older release wrote over the text alone, on a handbook whose
    checks run scripts. Its text still matches, so nothing a reader could compare has
    moved, and calling it MISMATCH would raise CONTRACT-DRIFT across every such handbook
    the day this release installs, with `--restamp` as the only way out, the gesture that
    also blesses real drift. So it is written under plain `--execute`, like a first stamp:
    the script half never had a prior claim. The cost is stated, not hidden: an edit made
    to a script before its first folded stamp cannot be detected, because nothing recorded
    those bytes.
    """
    if not ORG_RECORD_RX.search(body):
        return "NO-RECORD", None, None
    scripts = declared_scripts(body, root)
    want = contract_digest(body, root, scripts)
    if want is None:
        # A DELEGATING TIER HAS NOTHING TO HASH, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN, NOT A DEFECT.
        # `handbook-templates.md` § Lead: `tier: 2` carries "no `## Procedure` — a
        # coordinator's job is judgment", and `wf-conform` exempts the section for
        # tier 1 AND 2 for the same reason. Reporting those as NO-SECTIONS made
        # INV-STAMP read "no-sections 3" — three alarms for three correct handbooks,
        # next to a counter whose other values are all failures. They get their own
        # state so the count means what it says, and NO-SECTIONS keeps meaning what it
        # was for: an IC handbook that is genuinely missing a section it must carry.
        # No hash pair is invented for a Lead; a Lead has no procedure contract.
        m = TIER_RX.search(body)
        if m and m.group(1) in ("1", "2"):
            return "N/A", None, None
        return "NO-SECTIONS", None, None
    m = STAMP_LINE_RX.search(body)
    if m is None:
        return "UNSTAMPED", want, None          # engineering-lead's case: no line
    recorded = m.group(1)
    hit = DIGEST_RX.match(recorded)
    if not hit:
        return "UNSTAMPED", want, recorded      # the placeholder, or any non-digest
    if hit.group(1) == want:
        return "OK", want, recorded
    if scripts and hit.group(1) == contract_digest(body, None, []):
        return "PRE-FOLD", want, recorded
    return "MISMATCH", want, recorded


def write_stamp(body, digest):
    """Return body with the stamp line set to `digest`, or None if it cannot be placed.

    Replaces the line in place when one exists. When none does, inserts it where the
    template puts it — after `owns-records:`, else immediately before `ORG-RECORD END`
    — so a handbook that never had the line ends up shaped like one that did. The line
    is NEVER written outside the ORG-RECORD block: the block is what marks the fields
    as machine-owned and safe to replace.
    """
    line = f"<!-- contract-stamp: sha256:{digest} -->"
    if STAMP_LINE_RX.search(body):
        return STAMP_LINE_RX.sub(lambda _m: line, body, count=1)
    anchor = OWNS_RECORDS_RX.search(body)
    if anchor:
        return body[:anchor.end()] + "\n" + line + body[anchor.end():]
    end = ORG_RECORD_END_RX.search(body)
    if end:
        return body[:end.start()] + line + "\n" + body[end.start():]
    return None


# ── the `.directives.sha` half ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CANONICAL GRAMMAR, COPIED VERBATIM FROM `wf-conform` AND `wf-protect-directives`,
# NOT IMPORTED — the same rule the ORG_RECORD copies above follow, and `bin/check`
# cross-tests all three. A generator whose rows its own readers cannot parse is the
# inherited incident `checksums.md` opens by citing.
BLOCK_RX = re.compile(
    r"^<!--\s*origin:\s*user\b[^>]*immutable:\s*true[^>]*-->$(.*?)^<!--\s*/origin\s*-->$",
    re.S | re.M)
ROW_RX = re.compile(r"^\s*([0-9a-f]{64})\s+(\S+):directive:(\d+)\s*$", re.I)

DIRECTIVES_REL = os.path.join(".claude", "workforce", ".directives.sha")

SIDECAR_HEADER = (
    "# Sacred-block digests. Regenerated by `/workforce checksums --execute`.\n"
    "# `wf-protect-directives` compares every edit against these; a mismatch is UNPROTECTED.\n"
)


_SIBLINGS = {}


def _load_sibling(name):
    """Import a sibling shipped script by path, without leaving bytecode in `bin/`.
    Loaded once per process: the contract half asks for two siblings per handbook."""
    if name in _SIBLINGS:
        return _SIBLINGS[name]
    import importlib.machinery
    import importlib.util
    _prev = sys.dont_write_bytecode
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), name)
    mod_name = name.replace("-", "_")
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(mod_name, path)
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(mod_name, loader)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    try:
        loader.exec_module(mod)
    finally:
        sys.dont_write_bytecode = _prev
    _SIBLINGS[name] = mod
    return mod


def scan_blocks(root, unreadable):
    """{`<relpath>:directive:<N>`: sha256} over every sacred block in scope.

    THE SCOPE IS `wf-census.sacred_scope`, the one set `wf-conform` also demands rows over.
    Every `.md` under `.claude/` counts, except the run scratch and whatever `.censusignore`
    declares, read through the census's own matcher. This used to be four roots and a private
    copy of that matcher, and it could not write a row for a block conform required. Customer
    DEF-Q-006 (2026-09-15): an archive copy under `.claude/workforce/migrated/`. The set is
    still a SUPERSET of what `wf-protect-directives` guards (`/.claude/agents/`,
    `/.claude/workforce/directives/`, any `SKILL.md` in the project), so the hook can never
    fire on a block with no row. Personnel records stay in scope, because DEF and ORG files
    quote the user verbatim.

    The index is 1-based and per FILE, matching how `wf-conform` reads a row back
    (`found[i - 1]`). A file whose block ORDER changes therefore changes the digests
    of the rows below the move, which is correct: the row names a position, and a
    reordered sacred block is a change to what that position holds.
    """
    rows = {}
    files, _skipped = _load_sibling("wf-census").sacred_scope(root)
    for path in files:
        try:
            body = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:
            unreadable.append((path, type(exc).__name__))
            continue
        rel = os.path.relpath(path, root).replace(os.sep, "/")
        for i, m in enumerate(BLOCK_RX.finditer(body), 1):
            rows[f"{rel}:directive:{i}"] = hashlib.sha256(
                normalize(m.group(1)).encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()
    return rows


def read_sidecar(path, malformed):
    """{key: sha} from an existing sidecar. Readers are liberal, so an unparseable
    data line is COLLECTED and reported rather than dropped — a row nobody can read is
    a coverage gap, and a silently-skipped one is the sidecar that reports clean about
    blocks it never examined (`checksums.md`, the inherited incident)."""
    rows = {}
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return rows
    try:
        body = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return rows
    for line in body.splitlines():
        if not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith("#"):
            continue
        m = ROW_RX.match(line)
        if m:
            rows[m.group(2) + ":directive:" + m.group(3)] = m.group(1).lower()
        else:
            malformed.append(line.strip()[:70])
    return rows


def stamp_directives(root, execute, restamp):
    """Report or write `.directives.sha`. Returns a process exit code.

    THE MISMATCH RULE IS THE CONTRACT HALF'S, UNCHANGED (`checksums.md` § On
    mismatch): a row whose digest no longer matches is either an authorized amendment
    that did not restamp or an edit nobody approved, and the hash cannot tell them
    apart. `--execute` writes rows that do not exist yet — a block with no row has no
    prior claim to erase — and REFUSES every differing row, exiting 1. `--restamp` is
    the separate gesture, and the amendment is what authorizes it.

    THE MIRRORS AND THE PINS RUN FIRST, and in that order, because each one's write is
    the next one's input: an appended mirror block is a new sacred block the sidecar must
    then cover, and an appended mirror is a moved file every pin on it must then follow.
    Their rules are `mirror_pass`'s and `pins_pass`'s.
    """
    print(f"wf-stamp --directives — {root}")
    agents = os.path.join(root, ".claude", "agents")
    links_before = symlink_census(agents) if os.path.isdir(agents) else {}
    mirrors, moved = mirror_pass(root, execute)
    pins = pins_pass(root, execute, restamp, moved)

    path = os.path.join(root, DIRECTIVES_REL)
    unreadable, malformed = [], []
    found = scan_blocks(root, unreadable)
    recorded = read_sidecar(path, malformed)

    fresh = sorted(k for k in found if k not in recorded)
    drift = sorted(k for k in found if k in recorded and recorded[k] != found[k])
    agree = sorted(k for k in found if k in recorded and recorded[k] == found[k])
    orphan = sorted(k for k in recorded if k not in found)

    for key, kind in ((k, "UNPROTECTED") for k in fresh):
        print(f"  ~ {kind}   {key}  no row — the hook cannot verify this block")
    for key in drift:
        print(f"  ✗ DRIFT        {key}")
        print(f"      recorded sha256:{recorded[key][:12]}… · recomputed sha256:{found[key][:12]}…")
    for key in orphan:
        print(f"  ! ORPHAN ROW   {key}  the sidecar names a block that is not there now")
    for bad in malformed:
        print(f"  ! MALFORMED    {bad!r}  canonical grammar: <sha>  <path>:directive:<N>")
    for pth, exc in unreadable:
        print(f"  ! UNREADABLE   {pth}  ({exc}) — NOT counted as covered")

    # A DRIFT ROW BLOCKS ITSELF, NEVER THE ROWS AROUND IT. The two findings are
    # independent, and `merged` below already keeps a drifted row at its RECORDED
    # digest, so writing the missing rows re-blesses nothing. Until 2026-09-17 one
    # drift row suppressed the whole write: display mode said "re-run with --execute
    # to write the missing rows", `--execute` then wrote nothing, and the reader
    # looped. Reach is every project at once, not one tree — any release that edits
    # workforce's own `## Directives` block drifts the sidecar row recording it, so
    # `checksums --execute` became a no-op everywhere until someone restamped by hand
    # (customer re-test of 1.64.0, 2026-09-17). This is `classify`'s PRE-FOLD lesson
    # above arriving at the directives half: a state every install enters on the same
    # day must not have `--restamp` as its only exit, because that gesture also
    # blesses real drift.
    will_write = fresh or orphan or malformed or (drift and restamp)

    if execute and will_write:
        # WRITE, THEN READ BACK WITH THIS SCRIPT'S OWN READER (`checksums.md` rule 2).
        # An ORPHAN row is dropped here and nowhere else: it names a block that no
        # longer exists, so nothing can ever re-hash it, and keeping it is a row that
        # reports coverage of nothing.
        merged = dict(found) if restamp else {**found, **{k: recorded[k] for k in drift}}
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        body = SIDECAR_HEADER + "".join(
            f"{merged[k]}  {k}\n" for k in sorted(merged))
        open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(body)
        back = read_sidecar(path, [])
        if back != merged:
            print("  ! wrote .directives.sha and read back rows that do not match what "
                  "was written — NOT counted; the sidecar is unchanged in effect and "
                  "this is the generator/parser disagreement checksums.md cites",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        print(f"  + wrote {DIRECTIVES_REL}  {len(merged)} row(s), read back and re-parsed")
        blessed = sorted(k for k in drift if restamp)
        try:
            ledger_append(root, [{"event": "sidecar", "file": DIRECTIVES_REL.replace(os.sep, "/"),
                                  "from": f"{len(recorded)} row(s)", "to": f"{len(merged)} row(s)",
                                  "authority": "--restamp" if blessed else None,
                                  "restamped": blessed}])
        except OSError as exc:
            print(f"  ! the ledger was NOT written: {exc.strerror}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        print("    the counts below are the state AFTER this write, re-read from disk")
        # RE-DERIVE FROM WHAT IS NOW ON DISK, never from what was found before the
        # write. The counts used to be computed once, at the top, so a run that had
        # just written the missing rows still printed `unprotected 1` — an audit
        # reading INV-DIRECTIVES after `--execute` concluded the write had failed.
        # `back` is this script's own reader applied to its own output, which is the
        # same measurement the read-back check above trusts.
        recorded, malformed = back, []
        fresh = sorted(k for k in found if k not in recorded)
        drift = sorted(k for k in found if k in recorded and recorded[k] != found[k])
        agree = sorted(k for k in found if k in recorded and recorded[k] == found[k])
        orphan = sorted(k for k in recorded if k not in found)

    print(f"\nINV-DIRECTIVES  {len(found)} block(s) in scope · covered {len(agree)} · "
          f"unprotected {len(fresh)} · drift {len(drift)} · orphan {len(orphan)} · "
          f"malformed {len(malformed)} · unreadable {len(unreadable)}")
    print(f"INV-MIRROR  {mirrors['mirrors']} mirror(s) with a live source · "
          f"{mirrors['blocks']} source block(s) · mirrored {mirrors['mirrored']} · "
          f"unmirrored {mirrors['unmirrored']} · appended {mirrors['appended']} · "
          f"conflicted {mirrors['conflicted']} · failed {mirrors['failed']}")
    print(f"INV-PINS  {pins['pins']} pin(s) in {pins['handbooks']} handbook(s) · "
          f"current {pins['current']} · stale {pins['stale']} · repinned {pins['repinned']} · "
          f"missing {pins['missing']} · failed {pins['failed']}")

    rc = 0
    if not execute and (fresh or orphan or malformed):
        print("Re-run with --directives --execute to write the missing rows. Until a "
              "block carries one, wf-protect-directives reports it UNPROTECTED on every "
              "edit and wf-conform can verify nothing about it.")
    if drift and not restamp:
        print("A DRIFT row is not written by --execute, and it holds up nothing else: "
              "every missing row above is written, and the drifted row keeps its "
              "recorded digest. A sacred block changed after it was stamped; confirm "
              "the change was authorized, then "
              "`wf-stamp --directives --execute --restamp`.")
        rc = 1
    # AN UNMIRRORED DIRECTIVE OR A STALE PIN FAILS THE RUN IN BOTH MODES. Unlike an
    # UNPROTECTED row, which costs a hook its check, each one is an employee working
    # without the user's words or STOPPING on them, and a report that exits 0 over that
    # is the detector nobody acts on.
    if mirrors["unmirrored"] and not execute:
        print("Re-run with --directives --execute to append the unmirrored blocks to their "
              "mirror, verbatim, and re-pin the handbooks that pin it.")
    if pins["stale"]:
        print("A STALE PIN STOPS its employee on every spawn. Where this run did not move "
              "the file, confirm the change to it was authorized, then "
              "`wf-stamp --directives --execute --restamp`.")
    if (mirrors["unmirrored"] or mirrors["conflicted"] or mirrors["failed"]
            or pins["stale"] or pins["missing"] or pins["failed"]):
        rc = 1
    if links_before and report_symlink_drift(links_before, symlink_census(agents)):
        rc = 1
    return rc


def symlink_census(agents):
    """{basename: resolved target} for every symlinked handbook in `.claude/agents`.

    THE DETECTOR SKILL.md RULE 3 PRESCRIBES AND NOTHING PERFORMED. That rule ends
    "`wf-census` already reports `symlinks` and `aliases`; record both before the
    first write and re-read them after, and a drop is the whole detector" — and
    `wf-census` only ever REPORTED the counts. No caller compared them across a
    write, which is the difference between a measurement and a guard.

    WHAT IS AND IS NOT THE HAZARD, measured 2026-09-08 rather than assumed. Python's
    `open(path, "w")` FOLLOWS a symlink: it truncates and writes the TARGET, so the
    link survives and the `AGENT.md` that owns the registration receives the edit.
    That is what rule 3 asks a bulk writer to do, and it is what this script already
    did before this census existed. The destroyer is the rename-in-place family —
    `sed -i`, `perl -i`, `find -exec`, every tool that writes a temp file and renames
    it over the target — which replaces the link with a regular file EVEN WHEN THE
    PATTERN MATCHED NOTHING. So this census does not defend against this script; it
    defends against the next hand-run sweep across the same directory, and against a
    future edit here that reaches for a rename-in-place helper. A guard that only
    watches its own author is not a guard.

    Returns a dict rather than a count so a RETARGETED link — still a symlink, now
    pointing somewhere else — is caught too. A count alone reports that case clean.
    """
    out = {}
    for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(agents, "*.md"))):
        if os.path.islink(p):
            try:
                out[os.path.basename(p)] = os.path.realpath(p)
            except OSError:
                out[os.path.basename(p)] = "<unresolvable>"
    return out


def report_symlink_drift(before, after):
    """Print INV-SYMLINK and return the rows that regressed. Empty is the good case."""
    lost, retargeted = [], []
    for name, target in before.items():
        if name not in after:
            lost.append((name, target))
        elif after[name] != target:
            retargeted.append((name, target, after[name]))
    print(f"\nINV-SYMLINK  {len(before)} symlinked handbook(s) before · {len(after)} after · "
          f"lost {len(lost)} · retargeted {len(retargeted)}")
    for name, target in lost:
        print(f"  ! LINK LOST    {name}  was a symlink to {target}; it is a regular file now. "
              f"The registration has stopped tracking the file that owns it, and the "
              f"content there is frozen at whatever this run wrote.", file=sys.stderr)
    for name, was, now in retargeted:
        print(f"  ! RETARGETED   {name}  pointed at {was}, now points at {now}", file=sys.stderr)
    return lost + retargeted


def record_floor(root, name, evidence, execute):
    """Record a handbook's measured length floor in `wf-conform.LENGTH_FLOORS_REL`.

    THE NUMBERS ARE MEASURED HERE, NEVER TYPED (DEF-Q-23, 2026-09-16). The row says a
    relocation pass stopped at this size, and `wf-conform` stops ordering the split while
    the handbook stays at or under it. A floor somebody typed could be any number, so the
    writer reads the handbook's lines and bytes itself, at the moment the pass ends.

    Refused, exit 2: no such handbook; not governed (no ORG-RECORD); a Lead or CEO, whose
    length `wf-conform` already observes without ordering anything; evidence that is not a
    file inside the project, because a floor with nothing behind it is an assertion; a floors
    file `wf-settings-apply.write_refusal` refuses (a symlink, or a path resolving outside the
    project), because a row appended there lands in a file this project does not own. Not
    recorded, exit 0: a handbook within both budgets has no split to stop ordering.
    The grammar, the path and the budget are `wf-conform`'s, imported.
    """
    conform = _load_sibling("wf-conform")
    base = name[:-3] if name.endswith(".md") else name
    hb = os.path.join(root, ".claude", "agents", base + ".md")
    rel_file = conform.LENGTH_FLOORS_REL.replace(os.sep, "/")
    print(f"wf-stamp --floor — {root}")
    try:
        body = open(hb, encoding="utf-8").read()
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:
        print(f"  ! REFUSED  {base}: {hb} unreadable ({type(exc).__name__})", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if not ORG_RECORD_RX.search(body):
        print(f"  ! REFUSED  {base}: no ORG-RECORD — workforce did not author it", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    tier = TIER_RX.search(body)
    if tier and tier.group(1) in ("1", "2"):
        print(f"  ! REFUSED  {base}: tier {tier.group(1)} — wf-conform observes this length and "
              f"orders no split, so there is nothing for a floor to stop", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    ev = os.path.normpath(evidence or "")
    ev_real = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(root, ev))
    if (not evidence or os.path.isabs(evidence)
            or not ev_real.startswith(os.path.realpath(root) + os.sep)
            or not os.path.isfile(ev_real)):
        print(f"  ! REFUSED  {base}: evidence {evidence!r} is not a file inside {root}. Cite the "
              f"report that measured the floor, by project-relative path", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    ev = ev.replace(os.sep, "/")
    lines, size = len(body.splitlines()), len(body.encode("utf-8"))
    ceiling = conform.DEFAULT_LENGTH_CEILING
    budget = ceiling * conform.MEASURED_BYTES_PER_LINE
    if lines <= ceiling and size <= budget:
        print(f"  . nothing to record  {base}.md  {lines} lines, {size:,} B — within {ceiling} "
              f"lines and {budget:,} B, so no split is ordered and no floor is needed")
        return 0
    today = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d", time.gmtime())
    row = f"| `{base}.md` | {lines} | {size} | `{ev}` | {today} |"
    path = os.path.join(root, conform.LENGTH_FLOORS_REL)
    if not execute:
        print(f"  ~ would record  {row}\n  Re-run with --execute to write {rel_file}.")
        return 0
    refusal = _load_sibling("wf-settings-apply").write_refusal(root, path)
    if refusal:
        print(f"  ! REFUSED  {base}: {refusal} — nothing written", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    try:
        old = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        old = conform.LENGTH_FLOORS_HEADER
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:
        print(f"  ! {rel_file} unreadable ({type(exc).__name__}) — nothing written", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    out, replaced = [], False
    for line in old.splitlines():
        m = conform.FLOOR_ROW_RX.match(line.strip())
        if m and m.group(1) == base + ".md":
            if not replaced:
                out.append(row)
                replaced = True
            continue
        out.append(line)
    if not replaced:
        out.append(row)
    new = "\n".join(out) + "\n"
    if new != old:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(new)
    # READ BACK WITH THE READER THAT WILL USE IT (`checksums.md` rule 2).
    floors, _bad = conform.read_length_floors(root)
    got = floors.get(base + ".md")
    if not got or (got["lines"], got["bytes"], got["evidence"]) != (lines, size, ev):
        print(f"  ! wrote {rel_file} and wf-conform read back {got!r} — not counted", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    print(f"  + recorded  {row}  ({rel_file}, read back by wf-conform)")
    return 0




# ── mirrors and pins: the directive half's other two questions ──────────────────
DIRECTIVES_DIR_REL = os.path.join(".claude", "workforce", "directives")
# A pin is a directives path and the sha after it ON THE SAME LINE of `## Directives`,
# in every shape real orgs write (`— sha \`sha256:<hex>\` —`, a bare hex, a trailing
# "binds you" clause). The template's own `<directives-sha>` placeholder carries no hex
# and is not a pin.
PIN_RX = re.compile(r"(\.claude/workforce/directives/[^\s`'\"()|]+?\.md)[^\n]*?\b([0-9a-f]{64})\b")


def _sha_file(path):
    try:
        return hashlib.sha256(open(path, "rb").read()).hexdigest()
    except OSError:
        return None


def directive_pins(body):
    """[(directives path, pinned sha)] from `## Directives`, in order."""
    sec = section(body, "## Directives")
    if sec is None:
        return []
    return [(m.group(1), m.group(2)) for line in sec.splitlines()
            for m in PIN_RX.finditer(line)]


def repin_body(body, rel, old, new):
    """Body with every pin of `rel` at `old` moved to `new`: the `## Directives` lines that
    name `rel`, and the ORG-RECORD `directives-sha:` line that carries `old`. Nothing else
    is touched, and a pin on another sha is not this function's to move."""
    out = body
    m = re.search(r"^## Directives[ \t]*[^\n]*$(.*?)(?=^## |\Z)", out, re.S | re.M)
    if m:
        seg = "\n".join(line.replace(old, new) if rel in line else line
                        for line in m.group(1).split("\n"))
        out = out[:m.start(1)] + seg + out[m.end(1):]
    span = org_record_span(out)
    if span:
        block = out[span[0]:span[1]]
        block = "\n".join(line.replace(old, new) if "directives-sha:" in line else line
                          for line in block.split("\n"))
        out = out[:span[0]] + block + out[span[1]:]
    return out


def repin(root, rel, old, new, execute=True):
    """Re-pin every governed handbook whose `## Directives` pins `rel` at `old` to `new`.
    [(name, None | the reason it was not written)].

    THE CALLER IS THE AUTHORITY, and the narrowing is what makes that safe: only a pin
    equal to `old` moves, and `old` is the sha the caller measured immediately before its
    own write. A pin that was already stale before that write is somebody else's change
    and is left for `--directives --restamp` to decide. `wf-companion`'s T2 extraction
    calls this (nsayka-wawa DEF-Q-003: an extraction moved `operating-principles.md` and
    `elder-anchor-curator`'s cold probe STOPPED on DIRECTIVE-DRIFT), and so does the mirror
    append below."""
    out = []
    for path in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(root, ".claude", "agents", "*.md"))):
        name = os.path.basename(path)[:-3]
        try:
            body = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:
            out.append((name, f"unreadable ({type(exc).__name__})"))
            continue
        if not ORG_RECORD_RX.search(body) or (rel, old) not in directive_pins(body):
            continue
        if not execute:
            out.append((name, None))
            continue
        new_body = repin_body(body, rel, old, new)
        _write_handbook(path, new_body)
        if (rel, new) not in directive_pins(open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()):
            out.append((name, "re-pinned and read back a different pin"))
            continue
        try:
            ledger_append(root, [{"event": "repinned", "file": _rel(root, path),
                                  "from": f"{rel} sha256:{old}", "to": f"{rel} sha256:{new}",
                                  "authority": None}])
        except OSError as exc:
            out.append((name, f"re-pinned, ledger NOT written ({exc.strerror})"))
            continue
        out.append((name, None))
    return out


def _source_blocks(root, skill_dir):
    """[(rel, first line, last line, block text with markers, normalized body)] for every
    sacred block in every `.md` under one skill directory, in path order."""
    out = []
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(skill_dir):
        dirnames[:] = sorted(d for d in dirnames if d not in (".git", "node_modules"))
        for fn in sorted(filenames):
            if not fn.endswith(".md"):
                continue
            p = os.path.join(dirpath, fn)
            try:
                body = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
            except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
                continue
            for m in BLOCK_RX.finditer(body):
                a = body.count("\n", 0, m.start()) + 1
                out.append((_rel(root, p), a, a + m.group(0).count("\n"), m.group(0),
                            normalize(m.group(1))))
    return out


def mirror_pass(root, execute):
    """Compare every extracted mirror with the live skill it came from; under `execute`,
    append a source block the mirror lacks. Returns (summary dict, {rel: (old, new)}).

    THE QUESTION NOTHING ASKED (odyssey-alive row 26). `.claude/workforce/directives/<skill>.md`
    is extracted once, and the user goes on adding directives to `<skill>/SKILL.md`. The
    sidecar stamps the SOURCE, so `INV-DIRECTIVES` reported those blocks covered, and the
    employee bound to the mirror never saw them: five on odyssey-alive (agenda, ynab) found
    by hand, and three more in `invest` measured on 2026-10-22.

    MIRRORED MEANS CONTAINED, compared on the normalized block body: a source block is
    mirrored when its text sits inside some block of the mirror. Equality alone calls the
    `operating-principles` split unmirrored on every org it ran on, because the mirror
    keeps the pre-split span and the source keeps the user's remainder of it.

    APPEND-ONLY, AND ONLY FOR AN ADDITION. A missing block is appended verbatim with its
    `file:line` citation, the template's `<!-- extracted: ... -->` shape. When the mirror
    also holds a block that no source block is contained in, the source EDITED or deleted
    something, and appending the new text would leave the old one beside it for the
    employee to obey. That is CONFLICTED: reported, never written, left to a person."""
    ddir = os.path.join(root, DIRECTIVES_DIR_REL)
    summary = {"mirrors": 0, "blocks": 0, "mirrored": 0, "unmirrored": 0, "appended": 0,
               "conflicted": 0, "failed": 0}
    moved = {}
    rows = []
    for mpath in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ddir, "*.md"))):
        skill = os.path.basename(mpath)[:-3]
        skill_dir = os.path.join(root, ".claude", "skills", skill)
        if skill.startswith("_") or not os.path.isdir(skill_dir):
            continue
        try:
            mtext = open(mpath, encoding="utf-8").read()
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:
            summary["failed"] += 1
            rows.append(f"  ! UNREADABLE   {_rel(root, mpath)}  ({type(exc).__name__})")
            continue
        summary["mirrors"] += 1
        mirror = [normalize(m.group(1)) for m in BLOCK_RX.finditer(mtext)]
        source = _source_blocks(root, skill_dir)
        summary["blocks"] += len(source)
        missing = [b for b in source if not any(b[4] in mb for mb in mirror)]
        summary["mirrored"] += len(source) - len(missing)
        if not missing:
            continue
        stale = [mb for mb in mirror if not any(b[4] in mb for b in source)]
        mrel = _rel(root, mpath)
        for rel, a, b, _text, _n in missing:
            rows.append(f"  ✗ UNMIRRORED   {rel}:{a}  sacred block not in {mrel} — the "
                        f"employee bound to the mirror never reads it")
        if stale:
            summary["conflicted"] += len(missing)
            rows.append(f"      CONFLICTED: {mrel} holds {len(stale)} block(s) no source block "
                        f"matches, so the source edited or removed a directive rather than only "
                        f"adding one. Not appended: the old text would stay beside the new. "
                        f"Reconcile the mirror by hand, verbatim.")
            continue
        if not execute:
            summary["unmirrored"] += len(missing)
            continue
        today = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d", time.gmtime())
        add = "".join(f"\n<!-- extracted: {rel}:{a}-{b} | by wf-stamp {today}, added to the "
                      f"source after the last extraction -->\n{text}\n"
                      for rel, a, b, text, _n in missing)
        new = mtext.rstrip("\n") + "\n" + add
        old_sha = _sha_file(mpath)
        try:
            _load_sibling("wf-ledger").atomic_write(root, mpath, new.encode("utf-8"))
        except OSError as exc:
            summary["failed"] += 1
            summary["unmirrored"] += len(missing)
            rows.append(f"      not written, the mirror keeps its bytes: {exc.strerror}")
            continue
        back = [normalize(m.group(1)) for m in BLOCK_RX.finditer(
            open(mpath, encoding="utf-8").read())]
        if any(not any(b[4] in mb for mb in back) for b in missing):
            summary["failed"] += 1
            summary["unmirrored"] += len(missing)
            rows.append(f"      appended and read back a mirror still missing a block — not counted")
            continue
        new_sha = _sha_file(mpath)
        moved[mrel] = (old_sha, new_sha)
        summary["appended"] += len(missing)
        rows.append(f"  + appended     {len(missing)} block(s) to {mrel}, verbatim, each cited "
                    f"to its source line")
        try:
            ledger_append(root, [{"event": "mirrored", "file": mrel,
                                  "from": f"sha256:{old_sha}", "to": f"sha256:{new_sha}",
                                  "authority": None,
                                  "blocks": [f"{r}:{a}" for r, a, _b, _t, _n in missing]}])
        except OSError as exc:
            summary["failed"] += 1
            rows.append(f"      appended, and the ledger was NOT written: {exc.strerror}")
    for row in rows:
        print(row)
    return summary, moved


def pins_pass(root, execute, restamp, moved):
    """Compare every governed handbook's `## Directives` pins with the files on disk.
    Returns a summary dict. Prints its own rows.

    THE CHECK odyssey-alive row 27 found absent. A pin that no longer matches is the
    employee's own STOP condition (`DIRECTIVE-DRIFT`), and until now only a cold probe ran
    it, after the fact. STALE is re-pinned under `--execute` only where THIS run moved the
    file (`moved`, the mirror append above), because the run that changed the bytes is the
    authority for the pin following them; any other stale pin needs `--restamp`, the same
    confirm-it-was-authorized gesture the sidecar's DRIFT rows take. MISSING is never
    repaired: there is no file to pin to."""
    summary = {"pins": 0, "handbooks": 0, "current": 0, "stale": 0, "repinned": 0,
               "missing": 0, "failed": 0}
    for path in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(root, ".claude", "agents", "*.md"))):
        name = os.path.basename(path)[:-3]
        try:
            body = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            continue
        if not ORG_RECORD_RX.search(body):
            continue
        pins = directive_pins(body)
        if not pins:
            continue
        summary["handbooks"] += 1
        for rel, pinned in pins:
            summary["pins"] += 1
            fp = os.path.join(root, rel)
            inside = os.path.realpath(fp).startswith(os.path.realpath(root) + os.sep)
            cur = _sha_file(fp) if inside else None
            if cur == pinned:
                summary["current"] += 1
                continue
            if cur is None:
                summary["missing"] += 1
                print(f"  ✗ PIN MISSING  {name}  {rel} — no such file in this project; its "
                      f"`## Directives` STOP fires on every spawn")
                continue
            ours = rel in moved and moved[rel][0] == pinned
            if not execute or not (ours or restamp):
                summary["stale"] += 1
                print(f"  ✗ STALE PIN    {name}  {rel}  pinned sha256:{pinned[:12]}… · file "
                      f"sha256:{cur[:12]}…" + ("" if ours or restamp else
                      "  (moved before this run; --restamp re-pins it once the change is "
                      "confirmed authorized)"))
                continue
            new_body = repin_body(body, rel, pinned, cur)
            _write_handbook(path, new_body)
            body = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
            if (rel, cur) not in directive_pins(body):
                summary["failed"] += 1
                print(f"  ! {name}  re-pinned {rel} and read back a different pin — not counted")
                continue
            try:
                ledger_append(root, [{"event": "repinned", "file": _rel(root, path),
                                      "from": f"{rel} sha256:{pinned}",
                                      "to": f"{rel} sha256:{cur}",
                                      "authority": None if ours else "--restamp"}])
            except OSError as exc:
                summary["failed"] += 1
                print(f"  ! {name}  re-pinned, and the ledger was NOT written: {exc.strerror}")
                continue
            summary["repinned"] += 1
            print(f"  + repinned     {name}  {rel}  sha256:{pinned[:12]}… -> sha256:{cur[:12]}…"
                  + ("  (this run moved it)" if ours else "  (--restamp)"))
    return summary


# ── which handbooks a run touches ────────────────────────────────────────────────
NAME_RX = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$")


def staged_paths(root, name):
    """Every place a staged draft of `name` may be, canonical first.

    `staging/agents/<name>.md` is where `hire.md` T1 writes and what `wf-conform` pairs
    against the registered file. `staging/<name>/HANDBOOK.md` is the layout this script
    alone used to read (odyssey-alive PL-U3); older orgs still carry it, so both are read.
    """
    base = os.path.join(root, ".claude", "workforce", "staging")
    return [os.path.join(base, "agents", name + ".md"),
            os.path.join(base, name, "HANDBOOK.md")]


def _rel(root, path):
    return os.path.relpath(path, root).replace(os.sep, "/")


def select_targets(root, names):
    """([(name, path, kind)], [unresolved names]). `kind` is `registered` or `staged`.

    No `--agent`: every `.claude/agents/*.md`, as before. With it: only those names, the
    per-handbook target the hire carve-out needed (odyssey-alive PL-U2), so stamping a new
    hire no longer also upgrades every PRE-FOLD stamp in the tree. A named handbook that is
    not registered yet is looked for as its staged draft, which is where T1 stamps it
    (PL-U3). A name carrying a path separator is refused rather than joined.
    """
    agents = os.path.join(root, ".claude", "agents")
    if not names:
        return [(os.path.basename(p)[:-3], p, "registered")
                for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(agents, "*.md")))], []
    out, unresolved = [], []
    for raw in names:
        name = raw[:-3] if raw.endswith(".md") else raw
        if not NAME_RX.match(name):
            unresolved.append(raw)
            continue
        reg = os.path.join(agents, name + ".md")
        if os.path.isfile(reg):
            out.append((name, reg, "registered"))
            continue
        staged = [p for p in staged_paths(root, name) if os.path.isfile(p)]
        if staged:
            out.append((name, staged[0], "staged"))
        else:
            unresolved.append(raw)
    return out, unresolved


# ── the ledger ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# APPEND-ONLY, ONE JSON OBJECT PER LINE, AND WRITTEN BY THIS SCRIPT ALONE. It is the trace
# odyssey-alive PL-U4 asked for (a `--restamp` inside an order left nothing behind but its
# own stdout) and the history PL-U1 needed (nothing could say a stamp had ever existed).
# Each record names the file, what it held before, what it holds now and the authority,
# so a restamp is recoverable after the fact rather than erased by the act of making it.
LEDGER_REL = os.path.join(".claude", "workforce", "stamp-ledger.jsonl")
CONTRACT_EVENTS = ("stamped", "upgraded", "restamped")


def ledger_read(root):
    """([record], unreadable line count). Readers are liberal: a line that does not parse
    is counted and reported, never taken as evidence of anything."""
    out, bad = [], 0
    try:
        text = open(os.path.join(root, LEDGER_REL), encoding="utf-8").read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return out, 0
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return out, 1
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        try:
            rec = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            bad += 1
            continue
        if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("event") and rec.get("file"):
            out.append(rec)
        else:
            bad += 1
    return out, bad


def ledger_append(root, records):
    """Append `records`, then read the file back. Raises OSError when refused or when the
    file does not end with exactly what was appended (`checksums.md` rule 2).

    Refused when the ledger resolves outside the project: a symlinked `.claude/workforce`
    must not carry this project's record into somebody else's tree."""
    if not records:
        return
    path = os.path.join(root, LEDGER_REL)
    if not _load_sibling("wf-ledger").inside_root(root, path):
        raise OSError(errno.EPERM, "resolves outside the project root; nothing is written there",
                      path)
    at = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    blob = "".join(json.dumps({"at": at, **rec, "argv": sys.argv[1:]}, sort_keys=True,
                              ensure_ascii=False) + "\n" for rec in records)
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            fh.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
            if fh.tell():
                fh.seek(-1, os.SEEK_END)
                if fh.read(1) != b"\n":
                    blob = "\n" + blob
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(blob)
        fh.flush()
        os.fsync(fh.fileno())
    if not open(path, encoding="utf-8").read().endswith(blob):
        raise OSError(0, "read back a ledger that does not end with the lines appended", path)


def git_head_stamp(root, path):
    """The real digest git HEAD's copy of `path` carries, or None.

    None on every failure: not a repository, the file untracked or ignored, no `git`. An
    absent answer is never read as "never stamped", only as no evidence from git."""
    import subprocess
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "show", "HEAD:./" + _rel(root, path)],
                           capture_output=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    m = STAMP_LINE_RX.search(r.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
    return m.group(1) if m and DIGEST_RX.match(m.group(1)) else None


def prior_stamp_evidence(root, path, ledger):
    """Why an UNSTAMPED registered handbook is known to have been stamped before, or None.

    THE DISTINCTION odyssey-alive PL-U1 found missing. A never-stamped handbook and one
    whose stamp line was deleted after its `## Procedure` changed both classify UNSTAMPED,
    and plain `--execute` stamped the second as a first stamp, which erases the only
    evidence that the contract moved. Two records can say a stamp existed: this script's
    own ledger, and the committed copy in git HEAD. Either one is enough. Staged drafts are
    never asked: a draft being written at T1 is new by definition."""
    rel = _rel(root, path)
    for rec in reversed(ledger):
        if rec.get("file") == rel and rec.get("event") in CONTRACT_EVENTS:
            return (f"{LEDGER_REL.replace(os.sep, '/')} records `{rec['event']}` "
                    f"{str(rec.get('to', ''))[:19]}… at {rec.get('at', '?')}")
    head = git_head_stamp(root, path)
    if head:
        return f"git HEAD carries {head[:19]}…"
    return None


def release_state(ledger, rel):
    """The `amended` record that has no `released` after it, or None."""
    amended = None
    for rec in ledger:
        if rec.get("file") != rel:
            continue
        if rec.get("event") == "amended":
            amended = rec
        elif rec.get("event") == "released":
            amended = None
    return amended


def resolve_authority(root, arg):
    """(kind, project-relative path, text) or (None, the reason it was refused, None).

    THE GATE odyssey-alive PL-U4 asked for. `--restamp` erases the evidence a MISMATCH is,
    so it is accepted on exactly the two authorities the procedures name: an `AMD` record
    under `.claude/workforce/personnel/` (`amend.md` Step 7), which then reaches only the
    handbooks it names, or an audit's run directory under `.claude/workforce/work/audit-*`
    (`audit.md` Step 5-STAMP). An order dispatched by `/org` holds neither, so an employee
    cannot restamp inside one without citing a record that a later reader can open."""
    if not arg:
        return None, ("no --authority given. --restamp needs the AMD record that authorized "
                      "the amendment, or the audit run directory"), None
    real_root = os.path.realpath(root)
    real = os.path.realpath(arg if os.path.isabs(arg) else os.path.join(root, arg))
    if not real.startswith(real_root + os.sep):
        return None, f"--authority {arg!r} is not inside {root}", None
    rel = _rel(real_root, real)
    personnel = os.path.join(real_root, ".claude", "workforce", "personnel") + os.sep
    work = os.path.join(real_root, ".claude", "workforce", "work")
    base = os.path.basename(real)
    if (real.startswith(personnel) and base.startswith("AMD-") and base.endswith(".md")
            and os.path.isfile(real)):
        try:
            return "amd", rel, open(real, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()
        except OSError as exc:
            return None, f"--authority {rel} unreadable ({exc.strerror})", None
    if os.path.dirname(real) == work and base.startswith("audit-") and os.path.isdir(real):
        return "audit", rel, None
    return None, (f"--authority {arg!r} is neither an AMD-*.md record under "
                  f".claude/workforce/personnel/ nor an audit-* run directory under "
                  f".claude/workforce/work/"), None


def authority_covers(authority, name):
    kind, _rel_path, text = authority
    return kind == "audit" or f".claude/agents/{name}.md" in (text or "")


# ── the contract half ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def stamp_contracts(root, args):
    agents = os.path.join(root, ".claude", "agents")
    if not os.path.isdir(agents):
        # NEVER 0 ON AN UNREADABLE TARGET. A validator exiting clean on a tree it could
        # not open reports health it did not measure (`verify.md` § Handbook conformance).
        print(f"wf-stamp: no .claude/agents under {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    authority = None
    if args.restamp:
        kind, why, text = resolve_authority(root, args.authority)
        if kind is None:
            print(f"wf-stamp: --restamp REFUSED, nothing written: {why}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        authority = (kind, why, text)
    targets, unresolved = select_targets(root, args.agent)
    if unresolved:
        print(f"wf-stamp: no registered handbook and no staged draft for "
              f"{', '.join(unresolved)} (looked in .claude/agents/ and "
              f".claude/workforce/staging/agents/)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    ledger, bad = ledger_read(root)

    print(f"wf-stamp — {root}")
    if authority:
        print(f"  authority: {authority[1]} ({authority[0]})")
    if bad:
        print(f"  ! {LEDGER_REL}: {bad} unreadable line(s), not taken as evidence of anything")
    counts = {k: 0 for k in
              ("OK", "MISMATCH", "UNSTAMPED", "N/A", "NO-SECTIONS", "NO-RECORD",
               "STAMPED", "UNREADABLE", "STAMP-REMOVED", "UNRELEASED")}
    refused = []
    ledger_failed = False
    # BEFORE THE FIRST WRITE, not before the first write that happens to be reached.
    # Taken unconditionally — a review run writes nothing, and its INV-SYMLINK line is
    # still the record of what the directory looked like going in.
    links_before = symlink_census(agents)

    for name, path, kind in targets:
        label = name if kind == "registered" else f"{name} (staged {_rel(root, path)})"
        try:
            body = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
        except OSError as exc:
            counts["UNREADABLE"] += 1
            print(f"  ! UNREADABLE  {label}  ({exc.strerror})")
            continue

        state, want, recorded = classify(body, root)

        if state == "NO-RECORD":
            counts[state] += 1
            print(f"  . adopted     {label}  no ORG-RECORD — workforce did not author it, not stamped")
            continue
        if state == "N/A":
            counts[state] += 1
            print(f"  . n/a         {label}  delegating tier — a coordinator's job is "
                  f"judgment, so there is no ## Procedure to hash and no contract to stamp")
            continue
        if state == "NO-SECTIONS":
            counts[state] += 1
            print(f"  ~ NO-SECTIONS {label}  ## Procedure or ## Verification absent — "
                  f"nothing to hash; wf-conform owns that verdict")
            continue
        if state == "OK":
            counts[state] += 1
            print(f"  + OK          {label}  sha256:{want[:12]}…")
            continue

        evidence = None
        if state == "UNSTAMPED" and kind == "registered":
            evidence = prior_stamp_evidence(root, path, ledger)
            if evidence:
                state = "STAMP-REMOVED"

        if state in ("MISMATCH", "STAMP-REMOVED"):
            why = None
            if not args.restamp:
                why = "flag"
            elif not authority_covers(authority, name):
                why = "authority"
            elif authority[0] == "amd" and release_state(ledger, _rel(root, path)):
                why = "unreleased"
            if why:
                counts[state] += 1
                refused.append(name)
                if state == "MISMATCH":
                    print(f"  ✗ MISMATCH    {label}")
                    print(f"      recorded sha256:{recorded[7:19]}… · recomputed sha256:{want[:12]}…")
                else:
                    print(f"  ✗ STAMP-REMOVED {label}")
                    print(f"      {'placeholder' if recorded else 'no stamp line'} now, and "
                          f"{evidence}. A stamp existed and is gone, so the contract it "
                          f"described may have moved with it.")
                if why == "flag":
                    print(f"      the contract moved since this stamp was written, so the eval baseline "
                          f"is stale. FLAGGED, NEVER REPAIRED (checksums.md § On mismatch): a legitimate "
                          f"amendment and an unauthorized edit are indistinguishable from the hash, and "
                          f"re-stamping erases the only evidence. Run `review {name}`; where the "
                          f"amendment IS the authorization, `wf-stamp --execute --restamp "
                          f"--authority <AMD record> --agent {name}`.")
                elif why == "authority":
                    print(f"      --authority {authority[1]} does not name .claude/agents/{name}.md, "
                          f"so it does not authorize this one. Not written.")
                else:
                    rec = release_state(ledger, _rel(root, path))
                    print(f"      UNRELEASED since `{rec.get('authority')}` ({rec.get('at')}): the "
                          f"re-probe is not recorded, so the amendment is not finished. Record the "
                          f"PASS with `--released <probe report> --agent {name}` first (amend.md "
                          f"Step 6). Not written.")
                continue

        verb, future = {"MISMATCH": ("restamped", "restamp"),
                        "STAMP-REMOVED": ("restamped", "restamp"),
                        "PRE-FOLD": ("upgraded", "upgrade")}.get(state, ("stamped", "stamp"))
        if not args.execute:
            # PRE-FOLD COUNTS AS UNSTAMPED: its script half has never been stamped, and
            # plain `--execute` is what writes it, which is what that count's hint says.
            counts[state if state in ("MISMATCH", "STAMP-REMOVED") else "UNSTAMPED"] += 1
            had = "placeholder" if recorded else "no stamp line"
            if state == "MISMATCH":
                had = f"sha256:{recorded[7:19]}…"
            elif state == "PRE-FOLD":
                had = (f"sha256:{recorded[7:19]}… (text-only stamp from before check scripts "
                       f"were folded; the text still matches, so this is not drift)")
            print(f"  ~ would {future:9s} {label}  {had} -> sha256:{want[:12]}…")
            continue

        updated = write_stamp(body, want)
        if updated is None:
            counts["UNREADABLE"] += 1
            print(f"  ! {label}  ORG-RECORD has no END marker and no owns-records line; "
                  f"refusing to place a stamp outside the block")
            continue
        open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(updated)

        # READ BACK AND RE-PARSE WITH THIS SCRIPT'S OWN READER. `checksums.md` rule 2:
        # a generator that cannot verify its own output has not finished, and the
        # inherited incident it cites is a generator whose parser could not read its
        # own rows. `STAMPED` is never printed on a write that was not re-read.
        back, _, _ = classify(open(path, encoding="utf-8").read(), root)
        if back != "OK":
            counts["UNREADABLE"] += 1
            print(f"  ! {label}  wrote the stamp and read back {back} — not counted as stamped")
            continue
        auth = authority[1] if verb == "restamped" else None
        records = [{"event": verb, "file": _rel(root, path), "from": recorded,
                    "to": f"sha256:{want}", "authority": auth}]

        also = ""
        for sp in (staged_paths(root, name) if kind == "registered" else []):
            if not os.path.isfile(sp):
                continue
            staged = open(sp, encoding="utf-8").read()
            if contract_digest(staged, root) != want:
                also += f" (staged {_rel(root, sp)} has a different contract — left alone)"
                continue
            s_updated = write_stamp(staged, want)
            if s_updated is not None and s_updated != staged:
                open(sp, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(s_updated)
                s_old = STAMP_LINE_RX.search(staged)
                records.append({"event": verb, "file": _rel(root, sp),
                                "from": s_old.group(1) if s_old else None,
                                "to": f"sha256:{want}", "authority": auth})
                also += " (staged copy too)"
            elif s_updated == staged:
                also += " (staged copy already agreed)"
        try:
            ledger_append(root, records)
        except OSError as exc:
            ledger_failed = True
            print(f"  ! {label}  stamped, and the ledger was NOT written: {exc.strerror}. "
                  f"The trace this write owes is missing", file=sys.stderr)
        counts["STAMPED"] += 1
        print(f"  + {verb}   {label}  sha256:{want[:12]}…{also}")

    for name, path, kind in targets:
        rec = release_state(ledger, _rel(root, path)) if kind == "registered" else None
        if rec:
            counts["UNRELEASED"] += 1
            print(f"  ! UNRELEASED  {name}  amended under `{rec.get('authority')}` at "
                  f"{rec.get('at')}; no re-probe recorded since. An amended-but-unprobed "
                  f"handbook may not be delegated to (amend.md Step 6). Record the PASS with "
                  f"`wf-stamp --released <probe report> --agent {name} --execute`.")

    total = len(targets)
    print(f"\nINV-STAMP  {total} handbook(s) · stamped {counts['STAMPED']} · ok {counts['OK']} · "
          f"unstamped {counts['UNSTAMPED']} · mismatch {counts['MISMATCH']} · "
          f"n/a {counts['N/A']} · no-sections {counts['NO-SECTIONS']} · "
          f"adopted {counts['NO-RECORD']} · unreadable {counts['UNREADABLE']} · "
          f"stamp-removed {counts['STAMP-REMOVED']} · unreleased {counts['UNRELEASED']}")

    # THE HINT NAMES THE FLAG THAT WOULD ACTUALLY DO IT. `--execute` alone never
    # touches a MISMATCH, so printing it under a drift-only run sends the reader to a
    # command that will refuse — the "remedy with no producer" shape `verify.md`
    # § Hook wiring records itself shipping once.
    if not args.execute and counts["UNSTAMPED"]:
        print("Re-run with --execute to write the stamps. Until a handbook carries a real "
              "digest, CONTRACT-DRIFT cannot fire for it — org index, verify and review all "
              "compare a recomputation against a placeholder and find nothing.")
    if (counts["MISMATCH"] or counts["STAMP-REMOVED"]) and not args.restamp:
        print("A MISMATCH or STAMP-REMOVED is not written by --execute. Resolve it with "
              "`review <employee>`, or, where an authorized amendment is what moved the "
              "contract, `wf-stamp --execute --restamp --authority <AMD record | audit run dir>`.")
    symlink_drift = report_symlink_drift(links_before, symlink_census(agents))

    if refused or counts["UNRELEASED"] or ledger_failed:
        return 1
    # A LOST LINK OUTRANKS A CLEAN STAMP REPORT. Every handbook above can stamp OK and
    # the registration still be broken, so this cannot be folded into `counts` — it is
    # a different question with its own answer, and it fails the run on its own.
    if symlink_drift:
        return 1
    return 0


# ── the version stamps ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `audit.md` Step 5d item 3: "Set `workforce-version:` to the installed version, bump
# `handbook-version:`". No shipped script wrote either (nsayka-wawa DEF-Q-002, 2026-10-10:
# `--execute --restamp` set the contract stamps and left all 17 handbooks at
# `workforce-version: 1.61.0`, and the run set them by hand). Both fields live on the
# ORG-RECORD `hired:` line (`handbook-templates.md`), and they are read nowhere else.
WV_RX = re.compile(r"(workforce-version:[ \t]*)(\d+(?:\.\d+)*)")
HV_RX = re.compile(r"(handbook-version:[ \t]*)(\d+)")
HIRED_LINE_RX = re.compile(r"^([ \t]*<!--[^\n]*?\bhired:[^\n]*?)([ \t]*-->[ \t]*)$", re.M)


def org_record_span(body):
    start = ORG_RECORD_RX.search(body)
    if not start:
        return None
    end = ORG_RECORD_END_RX.search(body, start.end())
    return (start.start(), end.end()) if end else None


def read_versions(body):
    """(workforce-version or None, handbook-version int or None), ORG-RECORD only."""
    span = org_record_span(body)
    if span is None:
        return None, None
    block = body[span[0]:span[1]]
    wv, hv = WV_RX.search(block), HV_RX.search(block)
    return (wv.group(2) if wv else None), (int(hv.group(2)) if hv else None)


def set_versions(body, workforce_version=None, bump=False):
    """Body with `workforce-version:` set and/or `handbook-version:` bumped, inside the
    ORG-RECORD block only; None when the block has no END marker.

    An absent `workforce-version:` is added to the `hired:` line, where the template puts
    it and where `wf-conform`'s advisory tells a reader to add it; with no `hired:` line it
    gets its own line before `ORG-RECORD END`. An absent `handbook-version:` is NOT
    invented: nothing recorded what number it would follow from."""
    span = org_record_span(body)
    if span is None:
        return None
    s, e = span
    block = body[s:e]
    if workforce_version is not None:
        wv = WV_RX.search(block)
        if wv:
            block = block[:wv.start(2)] + workforce_version + block[wv.end(2):]
        else:
            m = HIRED_LINE_RX.search(block)
            if m:
                block = block[:m.end(1)] + f" | workforce-version: {workforce_version}" + block[m.end(1):]
            else:
                end = ORG_RECORD_END_RX.search(block)
                block = (block[:end.start()] + f"<!-- workforce-version: {workforce_version} -->\n"
                         + block[end.start():])
    if bump:
        hv = HV_RX.search(block)
        if hv:
            block = block[:hv.start(2)] + str(int(hv.group(2)) + 1) + block[hv.end(2):]
    return body[:s] + block + body[e:]


def _write_handbook(path, body):
    """Write through a symlink, never over it: `open(path, "w")` follows the link, which is
    the sanctioned form `symlink_census` above records the reasoning for."""
    open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(body)


def stamp_versions(root, args):
    agents = os.path.join(root, ".claude", "agents")
    if not os.path.isdir(agents):
        print(f"wf-stamp: no .claude/agents under {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    conform = _load_sibling("wf-conform")
    installed = conform.installed_version()
    want = conform.version_tuple(installed) if installed else None
    if want is None:
        # NONE MEANS NO WRITE. A script that cannot read its own release has no business
        # stamping one into a project's handbooks (`wf-conform.installed_version`).
        print("wf-stamp: the installed WORKFORCE-VERSION is unreadable; nothing stamped",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    targets, unresolved = select_targets(root, args.agent)
    targets = [t for t in targets if t[2] == "registered"]
    if unresolved or (args.agent and not targets):
        print(f"wf-stamp: no registered handbook for {', '.join(unresolved or args.agent)}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    print(f"wf-stamp --versions — {root}  ·  installed WORKFORCE-VERSION {installed}")
    counts = {k: 0 for k in ("versioned", "current", "newer", "adopted", "failed", "owed")}
    links_before = symlink_census(agents)
    for name, path, _kind in targets:
        try:
            body = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
        except OSError as exc:
            counts["failed"] += 1
            print(f"  ! UNREADABLE  {name}  ({exc.strerror})")
            continue
        if not ORG_RECORD_RX.search(body):
            counts["adopted"] += 1
            print(f"  . adopted     {name}  no ORG-RECORD — workforce did not author it")
            continue
        wv, hv = read_versions(body)
        have = conform.version_tuple(wv) if wv else None
        if have is not None and have == want:
            counts["current"] += 1
            print(f"  = current     {name}  workforce-version {wv} · handbook-version {hv}")
            continue
        if have is not None and have > want:
            counts["newer"] += 1
            print(f"  ! newer       {name}  workforce-version {wv} is AHEAD of the installed "
                  f"{installed}; left alone — this install is older than the one that wrote it")
            continue
        hv_to = hv + 1 if hv is not None else None
        detail = (f"workforce-version {wv or '(absent)'} -> {installed} · handbook-version "
                  + (f"{hv} -> {hv_to}" if hv is not None else "absent, not invented"))
        if not args.execute:
            counts["owed"] += 1
            print(f"  ~ would version {name}  {detail}")
            continue
        new = set_versions(body, installed, bump=True)
        if new is None:
            counts["failed"] += 1
            print(f"  ! {name}  ORG-RECORD has no END marker; nothing written")
            continue
        _write_handbook(path, new)
        back = read_versions(open(path, encoding="utf-8").read())
        if back != (installed, hv_to):
            counts["failed"] += 1
            print(f"  ! {name}  wrote the versions and read back {back} — not counted")
            continue
        try:
            ledger_append(root, [{"event": "versioned", "file": _rel(root, path),
                                  "from": f"workforce-version {wv} · handbook-version {hv}",
                                  "to": f"workforce-version {installed} · handbook-version {hv_to}",
                                  "authority": None}])
        except OSError as exc:
            counts["failed"] += 1
            print(f"  ! {name}  versioned, and the ledger was NOT written: {exc.strerror}",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        counts["versioned"] += 1
        print(f"  + versioned   {name}  {detail}")
    print(f"\nINV-VERSION  {len(targets)} handbook(s) · installed {installed} · "
          f"versioned {counts['versioned']} · owed {counts['owed']} · current {counts['current']} · "
          f"newer {counts['newer']} · adopted {counts['adopted']} · failed {counts['failed']}")
    if counts["owed"]:
        print("Re-run with --versions --execute to write them. This is the stamp audit Step 5d "
              "item 3 sets after it applies the shipped deltas, never before.")
    drift = report_symlink_drift(links_before, symlink_census(agents))
    return 1 if (counts["failed"] or drift) else 0


# ── amendment and release ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def record_amendment(root, args):
    """`--amended <AMD> --agent <name>`: bump `handbook-version:` and record the amendment,
    so the handbook reads UNRELEASED until `--released` records its re-probe."""
    kind, rel, text = resolve_authority(root, args.amended)
    if kind != "amd":
        print(f"wf-stamp: --amended REFUSED: "
              f"{rel if kind is None else '--amended takes an AMD record, not a run directory'}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if not args.agent:
        print("wf-stamp: --amended needs --agent <name>", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    targets, unresolved = select_targets(root, args.agent)
    targets = [t for t in targets if t[2] == "registered"]
    if unresolved or len(targets) != len(args.agent):
        print(f"wf-stamp: --amended reaches registered handbooks only; not registered: "
              f"{', '.join(unresolved or args.agent)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    print(f"wf-stamp --amended — {root}  ·  {rel}")
    failed = 0
    for name, path, _kind in targets:
        if not authority_covers((kind, rel, text), name):
            failed += 1
            print(f"  ✗ {name}  {rel} does not name .claude/agents/{name}.md — not recorded")
            continue
        body = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
        if not ORG_RECORD_RX.search(body):
            failed += 1
            print(f"  ✗ {name}  no ORG-RECORD — an adopted agent has no version to bump")
            continue
        _wv, hv = read_versions(body)
        hv_to = hv + 1 if hv is not None else None
        bump = f"handbook-version {hv} -> {hv_to}" if hv is not None else \
            "handbook-version absent, not invented"
        if not args.execute:
            print(f"  ~ would record amended {name}  {bump}; it then reads UNRELEASED")
            continue
        if hv is not None:
            new = set_versions(body, bump=True)
            if new is None:
                failed += 1
                print(f"  ! {name}  ORG-RECORD has no END marker; nothing written")
                continue
            _write_handbook(path, new)
            if read_versions(open(path, encoding="utf-8").read())[1] != hv_to:
                failed += 1
                print(f"  ! {name}  bumped and read back a different handbook-version — not recorded")
                continue
        try:
            ledger_append(root, [{"event": "amended", "file": _rel(root, path),
                                  "from": hv, "to": hv_to, "authority": rel}])
        except OSError as exc:
            failed += 1
            print(f"  ! {name}  ledger NOT written ({exc.strerror}); the amendment is not "
                  f"recorded", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        print(f"  + amended     {name}  {bump} · UNRELEASED until its re-probe is recorded "
              f"with --released")
    return 1 if failed else 0


def record_release(root, args):
    """`--released <probe report> --agent <name>`: the re-probe PASS that ends UNRELEASED."""
    ev = args.released
    real_root = os.path.realpath(root)
    real = os.path.realpath(ev if os.path.isabs(ev) else os.path.join(root, ev))
    if not real.startswith(real_root + os.sep) or not os.path.isfile(real):
        print(f"wf-stamp: --released REFUSED: {ev!r} is not a file inside {root}. Cite the "
              f"probe report that returned PASS", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if not args.agent:
        print("wf-stamp: --released needs --agent <name>", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    targets, unresolved = select_targets(root, args.agent)
    targets = [t for t in targets if t[2] == "registered"]
    if unresolved or len(targets) != len(args.agent):
        print(f"wf-stamp: --released reaches registered handbooks only; not registered: "
              f"{', '.join(unresolved or args.agent)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    ledger, _bad = ledger_read(root)
    evidence = _rel(real_root, real)
    print(f"wf-stamp --released — {root}  ·  {evidence}")
    failed = 0
    for name, path, _kind in targets:
        rec = release_state(ledger, _rel(root, path))
        if rec is None:
            print(f"  . {name}  not UNRELEASED — no amendment is waiting on a probe; nothing "
                  f"recorded")
            continue
        if not args.execute:
            print(f"  ~ would release {name}  amended under `{rec.get('authority')}`")
            continue
        try:
            ledger_append(root, [{"event": "released", "file": _rel(root, path),
                                  "from": rec.get("authority"), "to": evidence,
                                  "authority": evidence}])
        except OSError as exc:
            failed += 1
            print(f"  ! {name}  ledger NOT written ({exc.strerror}); still UNRELEASED",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        print(f"  + released    {name}  amended under `{rec.get('authority')}`, re-probed: "
              f"{evidence}")
    return 1 if failed else 0


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
        description=(
            "Write and check the records a handbook carries about itself.\n\n"
            "  (default)      contract-stamp: report; --execute writes a first stamp;\n"
            "                 --restamp --authority <AMD|audit run dir> overwrites a MISMATCH\n"
            "  --versions     ORG-RECORD workforce-version -> installed release, and\n"
            "                 handbook-version + 1, on every handbook stamped older\n"
            "  --amended AMD  record an amendment: handbook-version + 1, UNRELEASED until\n"
            "  --released R   records the re-probe PASS\n"
            "  --directives   .directives.sha rows, each extracted mirror against its live\n"
            "                 skill (appends what it lacks), and each `## Directives` sha pin\n"
            "                 against the file (re-pins what this run moved)\n"
            "  --floor H      a handbook's measured length floor\n\n"
            "Nothing is written without --execute. Every write is appended to\n"
            ".claude/workforce/stamp-ledger.jsonl."))
    ap.add_argument("--root", default=os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR") or os.getcwd())
    ap.add_argument("--execute", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--restamp", action="store_true",
                    help="also overwrite a MISMATCH — the authorized-amendment path; the "
                         "contract half needs --authority")
    ap.add_argument("--authority", metavar="PATH",
                    help="with --restamp: the AMD record (.claude/workforce/personnel/AMD-*.md) "
                         "naming the handbook, or the audit run dir (.claude/workforce/work/audit-*)")
    ap.add_argument("--agent", action="append", metavar="NAME",
                    help="only this handbook (repeatable). Unregistered: its staged draft")
    ap.add_argument("--versions", action="store_true",
                    help="set workforce-version to the installed release and bump "
                         "handbook-version where the handbook is stamped older")
    ap.add_argument("--amended", metavar="AMD",
                    help="record an amendment under this AMD record; needs --agent")
    ap.add_argument("--released", metavar="REPORT",
                    help="record the re-probe PASS in this report; needs --agent")
    ap.add_argument("--directives", action="store_true",
                    help="stamp `.directives.sha`, check every mirror and every pin, "
                         "instead of the handbook contract stamps")
    ap.add_argument("--floor", metavar="HANDBOOK",
                    help="record this handbook's measured length floor, so wf-conform reports "
                         "it instead of ordering the split again")
    ap.add_argument("--evidence", metavar="PATH",
                    help="with --floor: the project-relative report that measured the floor")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    root = os.path.abspath(args.root)
    modes = [m for m in ("floor", "directives", "versions", "amended", "released")
             if getattr(args, m)]
    if len(modes) > 1:
        print(f"wf-stamp: --{' and --'.join(modes)} are separate gestures; run them apart",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    # THE HALVES ARE SEPARATE GESTURES, never one sweep. They stamp different
    # artifacts with different mismatch consequences: a stale contract-stamp means an
    # eval baseline is old, while a drifted directive block means the user's own words
    # may have been reworded. Folding them into one flag would let a run that meant to
    # refresh an eval baseline silently re-bless a changed directive.
    if args.floor:
        return record_floor(root, args.floor, args.evidence, args.execute)
    if args.directives:
        if not os.path.isdir(os.path.join(root, ".claude")):
            print(f"wf-stamp: no .claude under {root}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        return stamp_directives(root, args.execute, args.restamp)
    if args.versions:
        return stamp_versions(root, args)
    if args.amended:
        return record_amendment(root, args)
    if args.released:
        return record_release(root, args)
    return stamp_contracts(root, args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
